  "notes": "The scales pulled hardest apart on 'killed' (fatality 100 but picturability only 45: the outcome is named and the physical event is invisible, no wound, no motion, no target) and on 'squeezed' and 'pulled', where the trigger action is the most precisely picturable thing in the list (80) while reading as calm and understated rather than graphic. 'discharged' is the inverse of 'blasted': the same physical event, but clinical register drops the violence score while fatality stays high. The aiming verbs ('aimed', 'leveled', 'levelled', 'trained', 'sighted', 'steadied') are all highly picturable with near-zero fatality, since nothing has yet left the barrel, and 'swung' is vivid and violent but the rifle-butt reading makes it far less lethal than any of the firing verbs. Adverbs, articles, pronouns, the possessive 'his', and the underscore strings were marked NOT_SCORABLE; bare finite verbs with no complement ('was', 'began', 'tried') were scored but given very low picturability."
